        <exercise id="exer-00002">
          <problem id="prob-00002">
            <para id="para-00007">Which one appears to be the bounciest? Which one appears to be the least bouncy? Be prepared to show your
        reasoning.</para>
            <para id="para-00008">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00002">
            <para id="para-00009">Compare your answer:</para>
            <para id="para-00010">The second ball is the bounciest. The balls were all dropped from the same height, and its bounces are the highest.
    The third ball is the least bouncy.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      
      
        <exercise id="exer-00003">
          <problem id="prob-00003">
            <para id="para-00011"> For each one, write an equation expressing the bounce height in terms of the bounce number <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>n</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
            <para id="para-00012">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00003">
            <para id="para-00013">Compare your answers:</para>
            <list id="list-00002">
              <item>Ball 1: <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>a</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>150</m:mn><m:mi>·</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac><m:msup><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mi>n</m:mi></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math> or <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>a</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>150</m:mn><m:mi>·</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>0.5</m:mn><m:msup><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mi>n</m:mi></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math></item>
              <item>Ball 2: <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>b</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>150</m:mn><m:mi>·</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mn>7</m:mn></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mn>10</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac><m:msup><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mi>n</m:mi></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math> or <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>b</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>150</m:mn><m:mi>·</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>0.7</m:mn><m:msup><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mi>n</m:mi></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math></item>
              <item>Ball 3: <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>c</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>150</m:mn><m:mi>·</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mn>5</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac><m:msup><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mi>n</m:mi></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math> or <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>c</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>150</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>0.4</m:mn><m:msup><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mi>n</m:mi></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math></item>
            </list>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      
      
        <exercise id="exer-00004">
          <problem id="prob-00004">
            <para id="para-00014"> Explain how the equations could tell us which one is the most bouncy.</para>
            <para id="para-00015">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00004">
            <para id="para-00016">Compare your answers:</para>
            <para id="para-00017">The bounciness of the ball is measured by the rebound factor, which is the base of the exponential expression. That
    is <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mfrac><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mfrac></m:mrow></m:math> for the first ball, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mn>7</m:mn></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mn>10</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac></m:mrow></m:math> for the second ball, and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mfrac><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mn>5</m:mn></m:mfrac></m:mrow></m:math> for the third ball. The
    larger this number, the bouncier the ball.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      
      
        <exercise id="exer-00005">
          <problem id="prob-00005">
            <para id="para-00018"> If the bounciest one were dropped from a height of 300 cm, what equation would model its bounce height <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>h</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>?
    </para>
            <para id="para-00019">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00005">
            <para id="para-00020">Compare your answer:</para>
            <para id="para-00021"><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>h</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>300</m:mn><m:mi>·</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mn>7</m:mn></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mn>10</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac><m:msup><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mi>n</m:mi></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math></para></solution>
        </exercise>
      
      <!--BEGIN ADD DESMOS INTERATIVE-->
      <!--START QUESTION # -->
      
        <exercise id="exer-00006">
          <problem id="prob-00006">
            <para id="para-00022">Graph, on the same coordinate plane, the equation for each ball that models the bounce height <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>h</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> after <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>n</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> bounces using the Desmos tool below.</para>
            
            <para id="para-00023">After you have finished graphing, select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00006">
            <para id="para-00024">Compare your answer:</para>
            <para id="para-00025">
              
                <media alt="Graph of the equation that models the bounce height \(h\) after \(n\) bounces for all 3 balls to predict the difference in the height of Ball 1 after 5 bounces.">
                  <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_05_13_06_02.jpg"/>
                </media>
              
          </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      <media alt="">
      <iframe src="../../media/desmos/a47b5bb4ba783be55a7067b919f165f14dcc36ce.html" width="500" height="400"/></media><!--END ADD DESMOS INTERATIVE-->
      <!--Q#7-->
      <exercise id="exer-00007">
        <problem id="prob-00007">
          <para id="para-00026">7. Use the graph of the equation that models the bounce height <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>h</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> after <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>n</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> bounces for all 3 balls to predict the difference in the height of Ball 1 after 5 bounces.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00007">
          <para id="para-00028">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00029"> The difference in height after 5 bounces is 5.5.
      Using the equation: 
      <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>h</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>150</m:mn><m:mi>·</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>0.50</m:mn><m:msup><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mi>n</m:mi></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math> and graphing <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>h</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>150</m:mn><m:mi>·</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>0.50</m:mn><m:msup><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math> The graph shows the height to be 4.6875 after 5 bounces <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>n</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>.

      This is consistent with the reasoning:
  </para>
          <list id="list-00003">
            <item>The height of the ball is decreasing by a factor of about
        <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac></m:mrow></m:math> after each bounce.</item>
            <item>After the 4th bounce, the height difference is 11. It should decrease by
        <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac></m:mrow></m:math> for each bounce after that. So, after 1 more bounce (the 5th bounce), it will decrease by a factor of about <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac></m:mrow></m:math>.
        The 5th bounce would be <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>11</m:mn><m:mi>·</m:mi><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>5.5</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>.</item>
            <item>4.6875 rounds to 5, and 5.5 rounds to 6.</item>
            <item>Take the average of the two numbers.
        <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>6</m:mn></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>5.5</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></item>
          </list>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Q#8-->
      <exercise id="exer-00008">
        <problem id="prob-00008">
          <para id="para-00030">8. Use the graph of the equation that models the bounce height <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>h</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> after <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>n</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> bounces for all 3 balls to predict the difference in the height of Ball 2 after 5 bounces.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00008">
          <para id="para-00032">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00033"> The difference in height after 5 bounces is 25.
      Using the equation: 
      <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>h</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>150</m:mn><m:mi>·</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>0.7</m:mn><m:msup><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mi>n</m:mi></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math> and graphing <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>h</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>150</m:mn><m:mi>·</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>0.7</m:mn><m:msup><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math>. The graph shows the height to be 25.2105 after 5 bounces <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>n</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>.

      This is consistent with the reasoning:
  </para>
          <list id="list-00004">
            <item>The height of the ball is decreasing by a factor of about
        <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mn>7</m:mn></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mn>10</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac></m:mrow></m:math> after each bounce.</item>
            <item>After the 4th bounce, the height difference is 35. It should decrease by
        <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mn>7</m:mn></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mn>10</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac></m:mrow></m:math> for each bounce after that. So, after 1 more bounce (the 5th bounce), it will
        decrease by a factor of about <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mn>7</m:mn></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mn>10</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac></m:mrow></m:math>. The 5th bounce would be <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>35</m:mn><m:mi>·</m:mi><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mn>7</m:mn></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mn>10</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac><m:mo>≈</m:mo><m:mn>24.5</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>.</item>
            <item>Rounding either 25.2105 or 24.5 results in the answer 25</item>
          </list>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Q#9-->
      <exercise id="exer-00009">
        <problem id="prob-00009">
          <para id="para-00034">9. Use the graph of the equation that models the bounce height <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>h</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> after <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>n</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> bounces for all 3 balls to predict the difference in the height of Ball 3 after 5 bounces.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00009">
          <para id="para-00036">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00037"> The difference in height after 5 bounces is 2.
      Using the equation: 
      <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>h</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>150</m:mn><m:mi>·</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>0.4</m:mn><m:msup><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mi>n</m:mi></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math> and graphing <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>h</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>150</m:mn><m:mi>·</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>0.4</m:mn><m:msup><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math>. The graph shows the height to be 1.636 after 5 bounces <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>n</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>.

      This is consistent with the reasoning:
  </para>
          <list id="list-00005">
            <item>The height of the ball is decreasing by a factor of about
        <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mn>5</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac></m:mrow></m:math> after each bounce.</item>
            <item>After the 4th bounce, the height difference is 6. It should decrease by
        <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mn>5</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac></m:mrow></m:math> for each bounce after that. So, after 1 more bounce (the 5th bounce),
        it will decrease by a factor of about <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mn>5</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac></m:mrow></m:math>. The 5th bounce would be <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>6</m:mn><m:mi>·</m:mi><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mn>5</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac><m:mo>≈</m:mo><m:mn>2.4</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>.</item>
            <item>Rounding either 1.636 or 2.4 results in the answer 2.</item>
          </list>
        </solution>
      </exercise></section>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!-- "Start "Are you Ready for More? click to reveal.  If you have more than one on a page, you will need to change the Data-fire/data-wait-for-events for each set.-->
      <section id="sect-00002">
        <title>Are you ready for more?</title>
          <section id="sect-00003">
            <title>Extend Your Thinking</title>
      
      <!--Start interaction.  If multiple interactions appear under "are you ready for more?, they should all have matching "data-wait-for-event", which should also match the "data-fire-event" for the button. Note in this sample, they are all RFM1-->
      <exercise class="RFM1" id="exer-00010">
        <problem id="prob-00010">
          <title>Extending Your Thinking</title>
          <para id="para-00038"> If Ball 1 were dropped from a point that is twice as high, would its bounciness be greater, less, or the
        same? Be prepared to show your reasoning.</para>
          <para id="para-00039"> Ball 4 is half as bouncy as the least bouncy ball. What equation would describe its height <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>h</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> in terms of
        the number of bounces <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>n</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>?</para>
          <para id="para-00040"> Ball 5 was dropped from a height of 150 centimeters. It bounced up very slightly once or twice and then began
        rolling. How would you describe its rebound factor? Be prepared to show your reasoning.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00010">
          <para id="para-00042">Compare your answer:</para>
          <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated" id="list-00006">
            <item>The same. The bounciness of the ball does not depend on the height from which it is dropped. This is why the
        successive rebound heights are modeled by an exponential function.</item>
            <item><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>h</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>150</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mn>5</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac><m:msup><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mi>n</m:mi></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math></item>
            <item>The rebound factor is very close to 0. Because the ball barely left the ground after hitting it, we could say
        that it lost almost all of its height with each bounce.</item>
          </list>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--End interaction-->
    </section></section>
  </content>
</document>